Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:02.629 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  one year ago today the city of wuhan  china was put  under lockdown as the coronavirus  continued to spread  for the next 76 days residents were  forced to stay in  while little information managed to get  out now a new documentary titled  76 days is shedding light on those early  chaotic moments  in the first hospitals to battle covet  19.  brook silva braga has an exclusive look  at the film  but a warning some of what it shows is  hard to watch  from its very first moments 76 days  is emotionally explicit  a nurse in a wuhan hospital begs to see  her dead father's body  her colleagues won't let her for fear of  contagion  director howe wu lets the ordeal play  out  for more than three minutes  why did you let it when you're so long  it was a creative choice to  let the audience experience and feel and  hear what was happening inside the  hospitals  to let them be emotionally shaken  and also moved how did you get in there  how did you get this footage  at the beginning of the lockdown there  was absolute chaos in the hospitals  and as long as a hospital chief granted  permission  to any reporter he or she can  could just really roam free inside the  hospital wu actually lives in new york  city  but last february he partnered with two  chinese journalists  embedded in wuhan hospitals huishi  chen and another anonymous photographer  each night they uploaded video for woo  to edit  it was really strange receiving footage  from wuhan in new york at that time  you know people still could go out to  eat go to restaurants  i was still meeting up with friends and  every night after coming home i would  start  reviewing the rushes from like two  cultural tents and and witnessing how  horrible  it was in wuhan almost no one in the  world  had seen footage like this in the middle  of february  that's absolutely correct we knew then  about the situation in wuhan came mostly  from images like these from chinese  state television  orderly hospitals with dancing patients  [Music]  this is what 76 days captures desperate  people  battling for a hospital bed  [Applause]  these were of course the very first  doctors and nurses in the world  to try to treat kovac 76 days portrays  them as heroes  discovering and navigating the cruelty  of the new virus  the image of the male nurse trying to  comfort an  old scared grandma who  was dying alone by herself that was  really powerful to me  but for a time wu feared none of this  footage would ever be publicly seen  as the virus spread and the chinese  government clamped down on negative  portrayals of its response  his two co-directors both chinese  citizens  stepped away from the project so for a  period of time we stopped collaborating  i was just basically sitting with this  footage and not sure whether i would be  able to  continue to make a film whether they  would let you use the footage  that yes that's right they had good  reason to worry  zhang jan a citizen journalist who  exposed bad conditions in wuhan on  social  media has since been sentenced to four  years in prison  so some viewers might be skeptical of  wu's motivation for removing  whistleblower interviews from early cuts  of the film  and avoiding political discussions in it  entirely  knowing you needed to get your  co-collaborators to agree to whatever  you made is it not fair to at least  describe this as a form of  self-censorship  the decisions you made and what to show  and what not to show i don't think so  because my co-director and i were  actually shot a lot outside the hospital  as well  we shot two whistleblower doctors it  just  didn't jive well with the other footage  we have which is  so powerful observational footage  the power comes in tiny details like  this plastic crate at the nurse's  station  filled with orphaned items that box of  phones  left over by the dead and also  especially that phone still  ringing  [Music]  31 messages unread absolutely and you  have to think what those messages are  it gives me shows even now when i think  about that image  against government regulation a nurse  collected the items  and then when the lockdown finally ended  called each  family  and returned what was left  loneliness and grief last spring that  would soon be shared  in every corner of the world for cbs  this morning saturday brooke silva  bragging  new york so interesting the decisions he  made as he put it  it's emotional yeah i mean just the  phones and returning  that that one moment you think of the  messages and people being alone we've  talked about this here we as you said  everywhere  people dying alone which they shouldn't  have done but knowing he saw this  footage before  the rest of us knew what was going to be  happening to us i mean  that in and of itself holding on to that  information  had to be torturous for him and the  heroes at the very very front line both  yeah  and here who were dealing with something  that nobody knew anything about right  at the very beginning all right today on  the one year anniversary mtv  is sponsoring digital screenings of 76  days allowing anyone to watch  for free if you want go to  76daysfilm.com i know it's tough to  watch

